WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.090 --> 00:00:08.858
Seeing Jennifer Lopez performing

00:00:08.858 --> 00:00:10.726
the role of a dancer in a movie

00:00:10.726 --> 00:00:13.029
may not seem that surprising.

00:00:13.029 --> 00:00:14.564
She's known for her moves.

00:00:15.531 --> 00:00:17.233
But knowing how to dance

00:00:17.233 --> 00:00:19.068
and knowing how to do this

00:00:19.068 --> 00:00:22.038
are two very different things.

00:00:22.038 --> 00:00:24.540
Turns out, even J-Lo needed some help

00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:26.309
to get ready to assume her role

00:00:26.309 --> 00:00:29.378
as an experienced pole
dancer in "Hustlers."

00:00:29.378 --> 00:00:31.581
That's where 10-year
veteran of Cirque du Soleil

00:00:31.581 --> 00:00:33.549
Johanna Sapakie comes in.

00:00:33.549 --> 00:00:35.218
Johanna Sapakie: By the time
we were done working together,

00:00:35.218 --> 00:00:37.553
even though it was just
a short period of time,

00:00:37.553 --> 00:00:39.255
she's throwing out these moves

00:00:39.255 --> 00:00:41.591
without even thinking about it anymore.

00:00:41.591 --> 00:00:43.960
Narrator: So how did Sapakie
get Lopez to the point

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:47.396
where she was doing upside-down
splits in just two months?

00:00:52.802 --> 00:00:55.304
Lopez took her training so seriously

00:00:55.304 --> 00:00:58.708
that she moved a portable pole
into each one of her houses

00:00:58.708 --> 00:01:01.644
in New York City, Los Angeles, and Miami,

00:01:01.644 --> 00:01:03.513
so that she could continue to practice

00:01:03.513 --> 00:01:06.048
wherever she happened to
be living at the time.

00:01:06.048 --> 00:01:08.818
While Lopez was already
in fantastic shape,

00:01:08.818 --> 00:01:11.053
pole dancing requires
certain muscle groups

00:01:11.053 --> 00:01:13.322
to be extraordinarily strong.

00:01:13.322 --> 00:01:15.424
And because there are no
body doubles in the film,

00:01:15.424 --> 00:01:17.760
she had to do everything herself.

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:19.795
So, to get her ready for the role,

00:01:19.795 --> 00:01:21.497
Sapakie first made sure that Lopez

00:01:21.497 --> 00:01:23.833
was exercising the muscle
groups that she'd need

00:01:23.833 --> 00:01:25.868
to pole dance like a pro.

00:01:25.868 --> 00:01:27.737
Lopez worked with her personal trainer

00:01:27.737 --> 00:01:30.740
to strengthen her core and upper body.

00:01:30.740 --> 00:01:32.408
She had to make sure that
she was strong enough

00:01:32.408 --> 00:01:34.243
to perform certain moves,

00:01:34.243 --> 00:01:35.511
like the inverted splits,

00:01:35.511 --> 00:01:37.580
where she's supporting
her entire body weight

00:01:37.580 --> 00:01:39.382
with just her arms and shoulders.

00:01:40.249 --> 00:01:42.885
As Lopez continued to
strengthen her body in the gym,

00:01:42.885 --> 00:01:45.254
she also began her
pole-dancing training sessions

00:01:45.254 --> 00:01:46.789
with Sapakie.

00:01:46.789 --> 00:01:48.724
Sapakie says that each training session

00:01:48.724 --> 00:01:50.760
always started with stretching.

00:01:50.760 --> 00:01:54.030
It's important to loosen the
spine, shoulders, and hips

00:01:54.030 --> 00:01:56.432
in order to provide a
wider range of motion

00:01:56.432 --> 00:01:57.833
for the undulating movements

00:01:57.833 --> 00:01:59.602
that are essential to pole dancing.

00:02:00.469 --> 00:02:03.406
After stretching, Sapakie
slowly introduced Lopez

00:02:03.406 --> 00:02:05.374
to the principles of pole dancing,

00:02:05.374 --> 00:02:08.110
such as basic spins and climbing.

00:02:08.110 --> 00:02:11.547
Sapakie: When we started, you
know, the trainings are slow.

00:02:11.547 --> 00:02:13.349
You do one movement at a time.

00:02:13.349 --> 00:02:14.617
You rest in between.

00:02:14.617 --> 00:02:17.386
If you're not used to a lot of rotation,

00:02:17.386 --> 00:02:20.089
getting the feeling of
spinning around the pole

00:02:20.089 --> 00:02:21.691
takes a little bit of practice.

00:02:21.691 --> 00:02:24.227
There's a lot of
conversation about, you know,

00:02:24.227 --> 00:02:26.028
how to do the movement

00:02:26.028 --> 00:02:28.431
and what the movement is.

00:02:28.431 --> 00:02:29.865
Narrator: Sapakie also emphasized

00:02:29.865 --> 00:02:32.768
improving Lopez's pole-dancing endurance.

00:02:32.768 --> 00:02:34.570
She knew Lopez was going to have to do

00:02:34.570 --> 00:02:38.174
her pole-dancing scenes
over and over and over again

00:02:38.174 --> 00:02:40.343
throughout the day when they were filming.

00:02:40.343 --> 00:02:42.111
Especially because her main routine

00:02:42.111 --> 00:02:44.547
was a full four minutes long.

00:02:44.547 --> 00:02:46.449
Sapakie: You're not just
doing one trick at a time.

00:02:46.449 --> 00:02:47.750
You're linking things together

00:02:47.750 --> 00:02:51.854
into, you know, two, three,
four minutes' worth of movement.

00:02:51.854 --> 00:02:54.757
She's doing takes from different
angles over and over again,

00:02:54.757 --> 00:02:57.126
and she has to nail it every single time.

00:02:57.126 --> 00:02:59.128
Narrator: Because Sapakie
also choreographed

00:02:59.128 --> 00:03:01.464
all of the pole-dancing
scenes in "Hustlers,"

00:03:01.464 --> 00:03:03.165
she knew exactly what moves

00:03:03.165 --> 00:03:05.901
Lopez was going to have
to perform for the film.

00:03:05.901 --> 00:03:08.337
There were several
particularly difficult moves

00:03:08.337 --> 00:03:09.639
in the choreography,

00:03:09.639 --> 00:03:12.742
but Sapakie was confident
in Lopez's abilities.

00:03:12.742 --> 00:03:15.177
Sapakie: There's one where
she actually wraps her legs

00:03:15.177 --> 00:03:17.913
around the pole and just
squeezes with her inner thighs,

00:03:17.913 --> 00:03:20.383
and that's all that
she's using to hold on.

00:03:20.383 --> 00:03:22.084
And that definitely took some practice

00:03:22.084 --> 00:03:25.888
because you really have to
engage those inner-thigh muscles

00:03:25.888 --> 00:03:27.690
in order to hold yourself on the pole.

00:03:27.690 --> 00:03:30.259
So that's not one that came on day one.

00:03:30.259 --> 00:03:32.061
We definitely had to work up to that

00:03:32.061 --> 00:03:34.697
in order to gain that
feeling and that strength.

00:03:34.697 --> 00:03:36.599
Narrator: Once Lopez
started to get confident

00:03:36.599 --> 00:03:37.867
with her pole dancing,

00:03:37.867 --> 00:03:39.335
the last thing for her to master

00:03:39.335 --> 00:03:41.137
was flipping upside down.

00:03:41.137 --> 00:03:43.639
Sapakie: You wanna make
sure that physically

00:03:43.639 --> 00:03:45.941
you're really capable
before you're inverting.

00:03:45.941 --> 00:03:47.910
It takes a lot of core strength,

00:03:47.910 --> 00:03:49.945
it takes a lot of grip strength,

00:03:49.945 --> 00:03:52.081
and it takes a lot of upper-body strength

00:03:52.081 --> 00:03:53.916
to physically pull yourself up

00:03:53.916 --> 00:03:56.919
and then maintain that position safely.

00:03:56.919 --> 00:03:58.788
Narrator: When Lopez was
first learning the move,

00:03:58.788 --> 00:04:00.923
Sapakie would provide a spot for her,

00:04:00.923 --> 00:04:02.391
making sure she was safe

00:04:02.391 --> 00:04:04.393
as she tried to master the movement.

00:04:04.393 --> 00:04:06.329
But it wasn't too long before she was able

00:04:06.329 --> 00:04:07.963
to do it on her own.

00:04:07.963 --> 00:04:11.367
Sapakie: She really became
comfortable with it quickly

00:04:11.367 --> 00:04:14.804
because of her work ethic and
because she was so diligent

00:04:14.804 --> 00:04:16.639
and determined to get it right.

00:04:16.639 --> 00:04:19.608
She was able to achieve
her goal by the end,

00:04:19.608 --> 00:04:21.110
which was turning upside down

00:04:21.110 --> 00:04:24.347
and ultimately looking
like she's been doing this

00:04:24.347 --> 00:04:25.815
her whole life.

00:04:25.815 --> 00:04:29.719
And that was really exciting
to see and really satisfying.

